875, 0.00786590576171875, -0.01480865478515625, -0.00801849365234375, -0.0257415771484375, 0.010345458984375, -0.03094482421875, 0.0049285888671875, 0.057159423828125, 0.00492095947265625, 0.007343292236328125, 0.0146484375, 0.0254058837890625, 0.033599853515625, 0.013275146484375, -0.0248870849609375, -0.02545166015625, 0.00994110107421875, 0.0166778564453125, 0.02099609375, -0.006877899169921875, -0.003597259521484375, -0.01190948486328125, 0.01428985595703125, -0.0034618377685546875, 0.01216888427734375, 0.0075531005859375, -0.00047397613525390625, -0.01238250732421875, 0.006317138671875, -0.0172271728515625, -0.00930023193359375, 0.0037097930908203125, 0.01070404052734375, 0.0228729248046875, 0.0186309814453125, -0.010040283203125, 0.002166748046875, 0.02716064453125, -0.0030078887939453125, 0.06011962890625, -0.044281005859375, 0.0273284912109375, 0.020843505859375, -0.039459228515625, 0.01171112060546875, -0.0116424560546875, -0.0031890869140625, 0.0233612060546875, -0.03948974609375, 0.0135040283203125, -0.0038394927978515625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are pivotal for achieving low-carbon heat supply. However, their operation faces several specific challenges: managing increasingly heterogeneous and time-varying renewable sources, integrating thermal storage, and handling complex meshed network topologies. This paper addresses the optimization of network operation over multiple time steps, where the objective is to minimize operational costs while satisfying nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints.\n\nWe build on a stationary, nonlinear network model that realistically reflects district heating operation by incorporating nonlinear pressure drops, thermal mixing at nodes, flow bounds, and temperature-dependent properties, as they are observed in actual systems. The model assumes steady-state conditions at each time step, which are a reasonable approximation for operational timescales in district heating.\n\nTo improve the computational efficiency of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for single time step optimization, several methodological advances are introduced. First, exclusion of cyclic flow refers to the elimination of redundant loop flows that do not correspond to any physically meaningful operation, thus reducing the solution space. Second, symmetry exploitation between supply and return networks leverages the structural similarities of the network branches, allowing for pruning of equivalent configurations. Third, temperature mixing constraint reduction means simplifying the set of constraints governing the mixing of flows at network junctions, thereby accelerating computation without significant loss of model fidelity. Additionally, we propose novel primal heuristics to find good feasible solutions rapidly and tailored branching rules to guide the search more effectively.\n\nThe methods are evaluated on both generated benchmark instances and real-world networks featuring cycles and multiple suppliers. On generated benchmarks, our approach increases the number of solved instances from 43 out of 100 to 92 out of 100, and reduces average runtime from 540 seconds to 210 seconds per instance compared to the baseline algorithm. In real-world cases, the improved algorithm consistently delivers solutions with a proven optimality gap of less than 1% within 30 minutes, whereas the baseline fails to guarantee solution quality within one hour.\n\nFor multi-period operation coupled by thermal storage, we investigate a time decomposition approach. This approach relies on the assumption that storage charging and discharging profiles can be independently optimized at each time step given a feasible initial state\u2014an assumption typically valid when storage is not at operational limits. Under such conditions, the decomposition yields provably optimal solutions. For example, on a representative small-scale network, the approach solves a 24-step problem in under one second, while the full monolithic problem remains unsolved after one hour. However, this methodology may be less accurate when storage continuously operates at its maximum or minimum bounds.\n\nIn conclusion, the proposed methodological improvements enable global optimization of complex district heating networks with significantly increased efficiency and reliability. Limitations include reliance on the steady-state assumption and potential restriction of the decomposition approach when storage is saturated. Future work could extend these methods to dynamic network models and explore more general coupling of storage over time horizons."}}